WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.382
Tomás is a programme manager.

2
00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:07.712
He runs two projects: highland water in Cajamarca,

3
00:00:07.712 --> 00:00:10.510
and youth skills in the Western Highlands.

4
00:00:10.964 --> 00:00:13.219
His home page starts with this week.

5
00:00:13.219 --> 00:00:18.479
Every activity due in the next few days is listed, with anything overdue at the top.

6
00:00:18.915 --> 00:00:23.808
On the right, each project shows indicators on track, progress against target,

7
00:00:23.808 --> 00:00:25.063
and activities done.

8
00:00:25.063 --> 00:00:27.133
Only approved figures count here.

9
00:00:27.551 --> 00:00:29.955
Below that are the reporting deadlines,

10
00:00:29.955 --> 00:00:32.913
so he knows when figures are due from his teams.

11
00:00:33.335 --> 00:00:36.564
Now he opens Activities to update the work itself.

12
00:00:37.015 --> 00:00:40.674
To do lists everything not yet finished across his projects,

13
00:00:40.674 --> 00:00:43.662
with the planned dates and where each one stands.

14
00:00:44.088 --> 00:00:46.671
The spring flow monitoring finished today.

15
00:00:46.671 --> 00:00:50.176
The team measured all eight springs, so he marks it done.

16
00:00:50.599 --> 00:00:52.665
He sets the status to Completed.

17
00:00:52.665 --> 00:00:55.698
Percent complete goes to one hundred by itself.

18
00:00:56.146 --> 00:00:59.363
The actual start and finish dates stay on the record,

19
00:00:59.363 --> 00:01:02.276
so anyone can see when the work really happened.

20
00:01:02.694 --> 00:01:03.105
Saved.

21
00:01:03.105 --> 00:01:06.260
The activity leaves To do and moves into Done.

22
00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:09.748
The pipeline works in Celendín are still going.

23
00:01:09.748 --> 00:01:14.065
The contractor reports sixty percent done, so he updates that too.

24
00:01:14.517 --> 00:01:17.869
A percentage tells the team more than a status alone.

25
00:01:17.869 --> 00:01:21.031
Everyone can see how far along the work really is.

26
00:01:21.444 --> 00:01:25.234
Deadlines puts every date in one place: reporting periods,

27
00:01:25.234 --> 00:01:28.697
activity end dates and calendar dates, soonest first.

28
00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:30.361
Overdue comes first.

29
00:01:30.361 --> 00:01:33.210
The third quarter figures are past their date,

30
00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.687
and some are still waiting for approval.

31
00:01:36.134 --> 00:01:37.740
Then the next thirty days.

32
00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.384
The pipeline works in Celendín are due in under four weeks.

33
00:01:41.827 --> 00:01:43.805
He also checks his indicators.

34
00:01:43.805 --> 00:01:48.814
The third quarter has no approved figures yet, so he looks at April to June,

35
00:01:48.814 --> 00:01:50.528
the last complete quarter.

36
00:01:50.976 --> 00:01:55.335
Households connected to a rehabilitated water system is off track.

37
00:01:55.335 --> 00:01:58.704
That is the conversation for his next team meeting.

38
00:01:59.134 --> 00:02:02.113
For a programme manager, this is the weekly check.

39
00:02:02.113 --> 00:02:05.390
What is due, what is late, and what has been delivered.
